      "text_raw": "We firstly have to determine\nthe exact biasing. We nor-\nmally require the output\nvoltage to be half the supply\nvoltage, when the input volt-\nage is half the supply volt-\nage. It does not have to be\nlike that, but this is a better\nway, for example, if several\nof these stages have to\ncascaded.\nIn this case, both transis-\ntors have the same V ,\nGS\nwhich is V /2. They also\nDD\nhave the same current. This\nis only possible if their W/L\nvalues are inversely proportional to their K∞ values. Since K∞ is usually twice as large as K∞ ,\nn p\nthe pMOST transistor has usually twice the W/L value compared to the nMOST.\nThe expression of the transistor current is then easily obtained, with V substituted by V /2.\nGS DD",
      "text": "We firstly have to determine the exact biasing. We normally require the output voltage to be half the supply voltage, when the input voltage is half the supply voltage. It does not have to be like that, but this is a better way, for example, if several of these stages have to cascaded. In this case, both transistors have the same V , GS which is V /2. They also DD have the same current. This is only possible if their W/L values are inversely proportional to their K∞ values. Since K∞ is usually twice as large as K∞ , n p the pMOST transistor has usually twice the W/L value compared to the nMOST. The expression of the transistor current is then easily obtained, with V substituted by V /2. GS DD"
